                    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
                    <div class="reader-page" id="reader-page-3">
                        <h4>Chapter 3: Spotting the 'Factory' Clinic</h4>
                        <p>Over 80% of "Skin Clinics" located in high-traffic tourist areas are managed by general
                            practitioners (GP) without formal specialist dermatological training. You can spot a factory
                            clinic by watching out for these three red flags:</p>
                        <blockquote>
                            <strong>Red Flag #1: Upfront Payments</strong><br>
                            If they refuse to let you speak with a medical doctor until you have signed packages and
                            swiped your card at the front desk, you are in a factory clinic.
                        </blockquote>
                        <p><strong>Red Flag #2: Extreme Price Dumps</strong><br>
                            If the price of highly consumable tip cartridges (such as Ultherapy or Shurink) is 50-70%
                            lower than the market average, the clinic is likely using counterfeit tips, reloaded
                            cartridges, or cutting corners on safety diagnostics.</p>
                        <p><strong>Red Flag #3: Abstract Review Metrics</strong><br>
                            If reviews of the clinic only mention the "kind translation staff" or "fast service," but
                            never name a specific doctor or comment on precise clinical parameters, ratings have likely
                            been manipulated.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
                    <div class="reader-page" id="reader-page-4">
                        <h4>Chapter 4: The Post-Travel Recovery Roadmap</h4>
                        <p>If you are returning to your home country immediately after a dermatological laser session in
                            Korea, follow this emergency recovery checklist to protect your barrier:</p>
                        <p><strong>1. The Post-Laser Flight Kit:</strong> Airplane cabins have humidity levels below
                            20%. Apply thick ceramide-based barrier creams and physical sunscreens (Zinc Oxide/Titanium
                            Dioxide) before boarding. Avoid chemical filters on fresh micro-wounds.</p>
                        <p><strong>2. Post-Inflammatory Hyperpigmentation (PIH) Prevention:</strong> Avoid direct sun
                            exposure for 14 days post-laser. If you develop dark spots (PIH), do not apply aggressive
                            chemical peels. Let the barrier rest and apply mild Cica (Centella Asiatica) or Panthenol
                            derivatives.</p>
                        <blockquote>
                            <strong>Emergency Assistance:</strong><br>
                            If you experience severe blistering, persistent swelling, or laser-induced burns after
                            returning home, contact our remote teleconsult lines through the Komicare App for immediate
                            guidance.
                        </blockquote>
